  "id": "0019-a-one-way-fit-is-a-min-filter-and-an-envelope-slope",
  "about": "fit_one_way(observations in receipt order), each observation a [source_ns, receipt_ns] pair of u64. delta = receipt - source in i128, truncated to i64 in two's complement. No observations fit nothing (expect none). offset_ns is the minimum delta and reference_source_ns the source of the first observation holding it. With fewer than 8 observations skew_ppb is 0 and skew_uncertainty_ppb is 18446744073709551615 (not estimated). Otherwise the first floor(n/2) observations are the early half and the rest the late half; in each, the first observation holding that half's minimum delta is its envelope point; span = late.source - early.source in i128; a span of zero or less estimates no skew; otherwise skew_ppb = (i64)((late.delta - early.delta) * 1000000000 / span) and skew_uncertainty_ppb = (u64)(2 * E * 1000000000 / span), each truncating toward zero, E being the spread of (delta - offset_ns) over every observation, the subtraction wrapping. The residual of an observation is delta - (offset_ns + drift), drift = (i64)((source - reference_source_ns) * skew_ppb / 1000000000) in i128 with the multiplication wrapping, the addition saturating and the subtraction wrapping; residual_spread_ns is their spread. A spread over values r is (u64)sqrt(sum(r * r) / n) in IEEE-754 binary64: each r converted to the nearest binary64, squared, and added to a running sum starting at 0.0 in observation order; the sum divided by n converted to binary64; the square root correctly rounded; the result truncated toward zero. That is the one floating-point computation, and its order is part of the contract. observation_count is n. bias_low_ns is 18446744073709551615 (unbounded) and bias_high_ns is 0 for every fit, because one-way data cannot separate a constant delay from an offset and delay is never negative. quality is ok when residual_spread_ns <= tolerance_ns, and degraded otherwise. A synthetic input stands for count observations: for i in 0..count, source_i = 1000000000 + i * period_ns, drift_i = trunc((source_i - 1000000000) * skew_ppb / 1000000000) in i128, and receipt_i = source_i + offset_ns + drift_i + floor_ns + jitter_ns[i mod len(jitter_ns)]. synthetic_clock_behind and synthetic_far_away_is_the_same_fit are one fit on purpose. The expectations were computed by the implementation that wrote the fits already recorded, not by the one these vectors check.",
